  "snippet": "#​672 — November 4, 2026 Web Version Postgres Weekly Postgres 19's REPACK: Built-In, Online Bloat Cleanup — Craig's favorite new Postgres feature is the one he thinks DBAs will care about most. REPACK folds VACUUM FULL a...",
  "coverImage": "https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/fuijuqlp9k2pt4psbneg.jpg",
  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​672 — November 4, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192394/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Postgres Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192360/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/fuijuqlp9k2pt4psbneg.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192360/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.snowflake.com\">Postgres 19's <code>REPACK</code>: Built-In, Online Bloat Cleanup</a></span> — Craig's favorite new Postgres feature is the one he thinks DBAs will care about most. <code>REPACK</code> folds <code>VACUUM FULL</code> and <code>CLUSTER</code> into one command and adds a <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> mode that keeps the table readable and writable, using logical decoding (a la <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192361/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>pg_squeeze</code></a>). He digs into its benefits and how to use it here.</p>\n<p>Craig Kerstiens (Snowflake) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>⭐ <b>Postgres 19 remains on track for November 19</b>, with RC2 due on November 12. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192362/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">Jonathan Katz has posted the draft press release</a> for review, and the <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192363/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">draft release notes</a> continue to expand.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192364/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"40\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/e3350811.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"160\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192364/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.enterprisedb.com\">Sub-Second Personalization on One Lakehouse</a></span> — Live demo: See how EDB Postgres AI delivers sub-second next-best-action on one copy of data in Apache Iceberg. Postgres, WarehousePG and ClickHouse share one catalog, with no pipelines between engines. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192364/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Watch the demo</a>.</p>\n<p>EDB <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #997 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192365/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"boringsql.com\">Getting Rid of a 4 Terabyte Table Efficiently</a></span> — How do you get rid of a 4 TB table on a primary with ten replicas with as little impact as possible? Radim compares the obvious <code>DROP TABLE</code> approach against batch deletes and a copy-and-swap, with the measured pros and cons of each.</p>\n<p>Radim Marek </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 In a similar vein, NexHealth has written up <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192366/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">how they moved 4 TB <em>out</em> of a live Postgres database</a> into its own Aurora cluster with no downtime.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Want to ease into contributing to Postgres? Paul Jungwirth is running a <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192367/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">patch review workshop</a> where small teams will get to review a patch, with committers like Robert Haas critiquing the reviews. You can <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192368/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">sign up here</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🏴󠁧󠁢󠁳󠁣󠁴󠁿 Jimmy Angelakos <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192369/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">recaps October's <em>Postgres Edinburgh</em> meetup</a>, where Elizabeth Christensen covered what's new in Postgres 18 and 19.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Supabase has opened a <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192370/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">public beta of OrioleDB</a> and a <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192371/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">private alpha of Multigres</a>. OrioleDB's alternative Postgres storage engine needs no <code>VACUUM</code>, but it's not production-ready yet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192372/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CloudNativePG is now a CNCF 'incubating' project</a>. Gabriele Bartolini tells the project's story from its 2019 start at 2ndQuadrant to ~247 million operator downloads today.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192373/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.pgedge.com\">Burnt TOAST: Why DBAs Dread Large Text Columns</a></span> — A tour of how Postgres stores oversized column values, with benchmarks showing why keeping the heap small can speed up scans and updates that leave those values untouched, and why fetching them can still be expensive.</p>\n<p>Shaun Thomas (pgEdge) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 Sticking to the TOAST theme, Franck Pachot ponders: <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192374/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">do TOAST's compression and out-of-line thresholds suit</a> typical JSONB document sizes?</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192375/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"now-next.nl\"><code>'server closed the connection unexpectedly'</code> Explained and Measured</a></span> — Chris reproduces connection failures across six clients on Postgres 13, 15, 17 and 18, showing why the same failure produces different diagnostics, and why a lost connection during a <code>COMMIT</code> can leave the outcome uncertain.</p>\n<p>Chris van Eijk </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192376/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Logical Replication Beyond One Writer</a> – A walkthrough of building multi-region replication, testing failover and handling schema changes, with a runnable Docker lab. <cite>Dimitri Fontaine</cite></p>\n<p>📊 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192377/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Benchmarking Vector Search in Six Database Engines</a> – Postgres and pgvector go up against MongoDB, MariaDB, AliSQL and Valkey. <cite>Evgeniy Patlan (Percona)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192378/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">How Replication Slots Can Fill Your Disk</a> <cite>Anugya Sinha (Microsoft)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192379/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">How Real is 'Autonomous Postgres'?</a> <cite>Vibhor Kumar (EDB)</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📺 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192380/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">New 4-hour freeCodeCamp course</a>: Watch a 1.6-second Postgres query drop to 10 ms with TimescaleDB continuous aggregates.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES AND CODE:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192381/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/okrdnkb2yf4sxr0mofwq.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192382/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgModeler 2.0</a> <em>(above)</em> – Billed as <em>\"the biggest release in the history of the project\"</em>, the database modelling tool gets a multi-scene canvas and Postgres 18 support, but moves import, diff and SQL execution to a paid 'Plus' edition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192395/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Melange</a> – Compiles Zanzibar-style, relationship-based authorization from <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192396/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">OpenFGA</a> models into PL/pgSQL functions, so a permission check is just a <code>SELECT check_permission(...)</code> away, with no separate service or store.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🌐 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192383/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PostGIS 3.7.0</a> – The geospatial extension (now targeting Postgres 19) adds coverage edges, Float16 rasters and faster <code>ST_DWithin</code>. Postgres 12/13 support is dropped, and the geocoder and address standardizer move to separate repos.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192386/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PostgreSQL Anonymizer 3.3</a> – Dalibo's data-masking extension fixes 12 vulnerabilities and existing users are urged to upgrade.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192387/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgAdmin 4 v9.19</a> – Fixes 15 CVEs. Server-mode users should upgrade, setting two new admin allowlists first.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192384/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres.js 4.0</a> – Node.js driver rewritten with a scheduler to stop slow queries blocking fast ones, SCRAM channel binding and two security fixes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192385/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">TimescaleDB 2.31</a> – Tiger Data's time-series extension gains a new integer compressor (<em>off by default</em> for now, but expected to be on by default in 2.32) that can make databases ~10% smaller and typical queries ~50% faster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192388/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_duckdb 1.2.0</a> – The DuckDB-in-Postgres extension moves to <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192389/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">DuckDB 2.0</a> and adds initial Postgres 19 support. It's <em>'not ready for production use'</em> for now.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192390/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_lion 0.3</a> – A bitmap-based inverted index type (inspired by <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192391/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">roaring bitmaps</a>, hence the name!) for faster counts, facets and <code>GROUP BY</code> on large tables.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192392/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Reylo 0.7</a> – Import plain <code>.sql</code> files into TypeScript with types inferred from your database. From the creator of <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/192393/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Zod</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}